@@ -94,6 +94,12 @@ Cloning a repository and building a custom sandbox image report progress while t

 A clone that is stopped this way is not retried, since a retry would wait on the same network. When the execution host reaches GitHub or Debian mirrors slowly, point git at a proxy (`git config --global http.proxy`) and use a nearby package mirror in the Dockerfile rather than raising these limits.

+### Private GitLab over HTTP
+
+An administrator can allow HTTP GitLab addresses on a trusted private network. Set `AGENTFLOW_GIT_EGRESS_ALLOW_CIDRS=192.168.50.10/32` in the platform service environment, or in `.env` for Docker Compose, and restart the platform. Use the GitLab IP or the narrowest CIDR it needs. If the API and workers run separately, give them the same setting. The platform and the execution environment must both be able to reach GitLab.
+
+In personal settings, add a **Git access token** with platform **GitLab** and host `http://192.168.50.10:8443`. Use the same HTTP address as the origin of the project repository, such as `http://192.168.50.10:8443/group/repo.git`. Verify the token before running the workflow. Keep this on a trusted network because HTTP carries Git and API credentials in plaintext.
+
 ### Git repositories over SSH

 Use Git SSH for GitLab.com, self-managed GitLab, Gitea, Gogs, GitHub, and other Git hosts that offer this transport. In **Project settings → Code repositories** or **Quick access**, enter the clone address, such as `git@gitlab.com:group/repo.git` or `ssh://git@git.example.org:2222/group/repo.git`, and select the existing default branch. GitHub HTTPS continues to use GitHub authorization or a PAT.
